# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

## Summary
Successfully integrated Vercel Web Analytics into the FluxDM web application following the official Vercel documentation for React/Vite projects.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files
- **src/App.tsx**: Added Vercel Web Analytics integration
  - Imported `Analytics` component from `@vercel/analytics/react`
  - Added `<Analytics />` component to the component tree inside the ErrorBoundary, after the Suspense block

## Implementation Details

The `@vercel/analytics` package (v1.6.1) was already present in the project dependencies, so no package installation was required. 

The Analytics component was strategically placed:
- Inside the `<ErrorBoundary>` wrapper to ensure proper error handling
- After the `<Suspense>` wrapper to avoid any loading conflicts
- At the root level of the app to track all routes automatically

This implementation provides:
- Automatic page view tracking across all routes
- Integration with React Router for seamless route detection
- Zero configuration required - works out of the box when deployed to Vercel
- Compatible with the existing Google Analytics setup (both can coexist)

## Verification

✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✅ Linter passed with `npm run lint` (no new errors or warnings introduced)
✅ TypeScript compilation successful
✅ No breaking changes to existing functionality

## Next Steps

Once deployed to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics tab → Enable)
2. After deployment, verify that `/_vercel/insights/view` requests appear in the browser's Network tab
3. View analytics data in the Vercel dashboard under the Analytics tab

The implementation follows React best practices and maintains consistency with the existing codebase structure.
